; SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
;
; CAN RX edge capture.  This is not the live sampler (see rx.pio) and it is
; not tested on hardware.  It stays beside the transmitter in the same
; 7-word budget so it can replace the oversampler later: 7 + 14 = 21.
;
; X is a free-running down-counter.  The steady loops decrement it every two
; cycles.  Any edge, either direction, falls into `cap` once:
;
;   in x, 31      low 31 bits of X, X itself is not modified
;   in pins, 1    the new pin level
;
; Software configures shift-left and autopush at threshold 32, so the pushed
; word is (X[30:0] << 1) | pin.  The IN base and the jmp pin are the same
; GPIO.  The RX FIFO is joined, 8 deep.
;
; `in x` reads X.  Only `out x` writes it.  Each edge path is four cycles and
; does not decrement, so a capture is two counts short of a free-running
; 2-cycle tick.  The ISR adds that constant if it needs cycle-accurate gaps.
;
; X is 32 bits and wraps about every 2^32 ticks.  A not-taken `jmp x--`
; writes 0xFFFFFFFF and does not jump:
;   * high: `ht` is the instruction before `high`, so the fall-through
;     returns to the high loop instead of walking into the next program;
;   * low: `jmp x--, low` is the wrap instruction, so the fall-through
;     wraps back to `low`.
;
; Start with X nonzero (0xFFFFFFFF) and the PC at `low` or `high` to match
; the pin.  The file begins at `ht`; that is not the idle state.  The wrong
; loop pushes one junk edge and then tracks.

.program can_rx_edge
ht:
    jmp x--, high       ; X was 0: fall through into high
public high:
    jmp pin, ht         ; pin is 0: fall into cap
cap:
    in x, 31
    in pins, 1
    jmp pin, high       ; pin is 0: fall into low
.wrap_target
public low:
    jmp pin, cap        ; pin is 0: fall through and count
    jmp x--, low        ; X was 0: wrap back to low
.wrap
